An EC2 instance is like a computer. Every computer has:
- CPU – Brain of the computer.
- RAM – Temporary working memory.
- Storage – Where data is stored.
- GPU – Handles parallel processing, graphics and AI workloads.


1. General Purpose (T and M)

Meaning: Balanced CPU, RAM and networking.

Imagine you are hosting a normal web application. It needs CPU to process requests, RAM to keep the application running, and storage for files.

It needs a balanced combination of resources.
- T family: Burstable CPU performance, suitable for small or variable workloads.
- M family: Balanced, more consistent general-purpose workloads.
Examples: t3.medium, m6i.large.

Real-time example: Jenkins server, small web application, development server.
2. Compute Optimized (C)

Meaning: More CPU processing power relative to memory.

Imagine you are running 1000 jobs that require calculations. The application spends most of its time processing data rather than waiting for storage.

You need more CPU power.

- C family focuses on CPU-intensive workloads.

Examples: c6i.large, c7i.xlarge.

Real-time example: Batch processing, data processing, high-performance application servers.
3. Memory Optimized (R, X, U)

Meaning: More RAM capacity.

Imagine you are running a large database. It needs to keep frequently accessed data in memory to respond quickly.

If RAM is insufficient, the system may use disk swap, which is generally much slower.
- R family: Memory-intensive workloads.
- X family: Very large memory requirements.
- U family: Very high-memory instances.
Examples: r6i.large, x2idn.large.

Real-time example: Large databases, in-memory caching, SAP HANA.
4. Storage Optimized (I, D, H)
Meaning: Designed for workloads that need high-speed local storage or high storage capacity.

Imagine you are processing millions of records and frequently reading and writing data to disks.

You need high storage performance.
- I family: High-speed local SSD storage.
- D family: Dense HDD storage capacity.
- H family: High-throughput storage.
Examples: i4i.large, d3.xlarge.

Real-time example: Data warehousing, distributed databases, log processing.
5. Accelerated Computing (P, G, Inf, Trn)
Meaning: Uses specialized hardware such as GPUs or machine-learning accelerators.

Imagine you are training an AI model. It performs thousands of calculations simultaneously.

A CPU can handle general-purpose processing, but GPUs and specialized accelerators can handle these parallel workloads more efficiently.
- P family: GPU computing, especially AI training and HPC.
- G family: Graphics and GPU workloads.
- Inf family: AI inference.
- Trn family: AI training.
Examples: p5, g6, inf2, trn1.

Real-time example: AI/ML, deep learning, graphics rendering.
6. HPC Optimized
Meaning: High-performance computing for complex calculations across connected compute nodes.

Imagine weather forecasting. A large simulation needs multiple computers to work together and exchange data very quickly.

HPC instances are designed for such demanding workloads.

Examples: hpc7g, hpc7a.

Real-time example: Scientific simulations, weather modelling, engineering simulations.

